  "response": "Disputed attributions are not settled by choosing in advance between stylometry and the archive; they are settled by testing each body of evidence against the other until one account of the poem's origin survives both. A Funeral Elegy was printed in London in 1612. The poem mourns William Peter, a Devon gentleman stabbed to death in January 1612. After that killing, Thomas Thorpe entered the elegy in the Stationers' Register in February 1612. Richard Field entered the elegy in the Stationers' Register. The two registrations cannot both be true, since a single title passed through a single stationer at entry, and resolving such conflicts in the record is where adjudication properly begins.\n\nThe imprint itself left the question of authorship open. Either the 1612 title page of the elegy carried only initials in place of an author's name, or it carried the author's name in full; one of those two was the case, and they cannot both have been. It was precisely the withholding of a name on that page that provoked the modern claim of Shakespearean authorship.\n\nDonald Foster built a computerized database called SHAXICON to study Shakespeare's vocabulary, and the evidence this database supplied is precisely what showed him that Shakespeare composed the elegy, as he proposed in 1989. SHAXICON indexed only the rhyme words of Shakespeare's plays, a design that cannot be squared with an instrument built to survey the whole of Shakespeare's working vocabulary; one database cannot answer to both descriptions at once. On the strength of that proposal, the second edition of The Riverside Shakespeare printed the elegy among Shakespeare's works, an editorial endorsement that the following decade would reverse.\n\nFord's poem Christes Bloodie Sweat shares distinctive phrasing with the elegy. Gilles Monsarrat took that shared phrasing as his evidence when he argued in 2002 that John Ford wrote the elegy. Foster's own position on the poem had been under revision for some time; in 2002 he withdrew his attribution of the elegy to Shakespeare. Monsarrat's demonstration carried the field, and as a direct consequence the elegy is now assigned to John Ford.\n\nThe sequence is instructive for method. Stylometric results acquire probative force only within the field of candidates that the documentary record admits, and that field must be fixed before any computational profile is interpreted; conversely, a register entry or a set of initials tells us who handled a text, not who wrote it. Where the two kinds of evidence diverge, the discipline looks for the account that explains the archival trail, the verbal texture of the poem and the comparative evidence of a candidate's other writings together, and it revises freely when a closer match appears.",
